RM(1)                     Funny CLI Manual                     RM(1)

NAME
    rm — Remove a file, because sometimes deleting things is the solution.

SYNOPSIS
    rm <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The rm command permanently deletes the specified file.

    It only removes files. If the specified path is a directory, rm
    refuses to remove it and tells you to use rmdir instead.

USAGE
    > rm <file>       # Deletes the specified file.

FUN FACTS
    - rm stands for "remove", not "really messed up".
    - Deleted files are not moved to a recycle bin.
    - rm refuses to remove directories, because rmdir has a job too.
    - Always check what you're deleting. The filesystem does not have feelings, 
      but you might.

EXAMPLES
    > rm notes.txt
        Deletes notes.txt from the current directory.

    > rm /home/user/old.txt
        Deletes old.txt using an absolute path.

    > rm missing.txt
        Displays an error because the specified file does not exist.

    > rm documents
        Refuses to remove the directory and tells you to use rmdir.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), ls(1), rmdir(1), cat(1), man(1)

    Remember, rm does not ask "Are you sure?"
    It simply trusts your questionable decisions.

